1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,039
Welcome to School Computer Science,
Explained. This is Intro to Computer

2
00:00:04,039 --> 00:00:08,471
Science, Unit 4, part one. It's time to
write real programs in Python, starting

3
00:00:08,471 --> 00:00:12,958
from your very first line of code. After
every lesson, we'll work through a full

4
00:00:12,958 --> 00:00:17,109
example step by step, and we'll finish by
putting the whole unit together.

5
00:00:18,283 --> 00:00:23,044
Time to write real code. We'll use Python,
one of the most popular programming

6
00:00:23,044 --> 00:00:24,448
languages in the world.

7
00:00:24,898 --> 00:00:28,781
It's used for websites, games, science,
and artificial intelligence.

8
00:00:29,931 --> 00:00:34,970
Here's a complete Python program. One
line: print, open parenthesis, quote,

9
00:00:34,970 --> 00:00:37,590
Hello, world, quote, close parenthesis.

10
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:40,408
Run it, and the computer shows Hello,
world.

11
00:00:41,558 --> 00:00:45,888
print is a function. It displays whatever
you put inside the parentheses.

12
00:00:46,338 --> 00:00:50,882
Text goes inside quotation marks. Text in
quotes is called a string.

13
00:00:52,032 --> 00:00:57,003
A program can have many lines. Python runs
them top to bottom, one at a time.

14
00:00:57,453 --> 00:00:59,778
Each print starts on a new line of output.

15
00:01:00,928 --> 00:01:06,134
A comment starts with a hash sign. Python
ignores everything after it on that line.

16
00:01:06,584 --> 00:01:09,890
Comments are notes for people: what the
code does, or why.

17
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:16,024
Small mistakes cause errors. Forget a
quote, or misspell print, and Python stops

18
00:01:16,024 --> 00:01:17,270
with a syntax error.

19
00:01:17,720 --> 00:01:22,061
The error message tells you the line
number. Read it carefully: it's a clue,

20
00:01:22,061 --> 00:01:23,032
not a punishment.

21
00:01:24,182 --> 00:01:28,128
Your turn. What does this program print?
print, quote, two plus two, quote.

22
00:01:28,578 --> 00:01:29,773
Pause the video now.

23
00:01:30,223 --> 00:01:35,624
It prints the text two plus two, not four,
because it's inside quotes. It's a string,

24
00:01:35,624 --> 00:01:36,196
not math.

25
00:01:36,646 --> 00:01:39,633
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

26
00:01:40,783 --> 00:01:45,679
Let's write a small program with several
print lines and a comment, and predict its

27
00:01:45,679 --> 00:01:46,564
output exactly.

28
00:01:47,714 --> 00:01:52,544
The problem: print a movie ticket on four
lines: a border, the movie name, the seat,

29
00:01:52,544 --> 00:01:53,752
and the border again.

30
00:01:54,202 --> 00:01:56,804
Also add a comment saying what the program
does.

31
00:01:57,954 --> 00:02:01,901
Here's the program. The first line is a
comment, so Python skips it.

32
00:02:02,351 --> 00:02:06,042
Then four print lines. Each one puts its
string on its own line.

33
00:02:06,492 --> 00:02:11,441
Notice the quotes. Everything inside them
is printed exactly, spaces included.

34
00:02:12,591 --> 00:02:16,858
Python runs top to bottom. Line one is a
comment: nothing happens.

35
00:02:17,308 --> 00:02:22,860
Line two prints the stars. Line three, the
title. Line four, the seat. Line five, the

36
00:02:22,860 --> 00:02:23,644
stars again.

37
00:02:24,794 --> 00:02:28,442
The output is four lines, in the same
order as the print lines.

38
00:02:28,892 --> 00:02:34,194
Common mistakes: forgetting a quote, or
writing Print with a capital P. Python is

39
00:02:34,194 --> 00:02:36,550
case sensitive, so both give errors.

40
00:02:37,700 --> 00:02:39,898
Your turn. How many lines does this print?

41
00:02:40,348 --> 00:02:41,542
Pause the video now.

42
00:02:41,992 --> 00:02:46,195
Two lines: A, then C. The B line starts
with a hash, so it's a comment.

43
00:02:46,645 --> 00:02:47,754
Next, variables.

44
00:02:48,904 --> 00:02:53,277
Programs need to remember things: a score,
a name, the number of lives left.

45
00:02:53,727 --> 00:02:54,858
They use variables.

46
00:02:56,008 --> 00:02:58,611
A variable is a name that refers to a
value.

47
00:02:59,061 --> 00:03:04,138
score equals zero creates a variable
called score, and gives it the value zero.

48
00:03:05,288 --> 00:03:09,384
The equals sign means assign. It doesn't
mean equal in the math sense.

49
00:03:09,834 --> 00:03:14,392
Python works out the right side first,
then stores the result under the name on

50
00:03:14,392 --> 00:03:14,911
the left.

51
00:03:16,061 --> 00:03:19,005
That's why score equals score plus ten
makes sense.

52
00:03:19,455 --> 00:03:24,127
Python takes the current score, adds ten,
and stores the answer back in score.

53
00:03:25,277 --> 00:03:30,805
Variable names can use letters, digits and
underscores, but can't start with a digit,

54
00:03:30,805 --> 00:03:31,976
or contain spaces.

55
00:03:32,426 --> 00:03:36,565
Good names describe what they hold. lives
left is clearer than x.

56
00:03:37,715 --> 00:03:42,792
Let's trace a short program. a equals
five. b equals a plus two. a equals ten.

57
00:03:43,242 --> 00:03:48,255
After that, a is ten, and b is still
seven. Changing a later doesn't change b.

58
00:03:49,405 --> 00:03:55,656
Your turn. x equals three. x equals x
times two. x equals x plus one. What is x?

59
00:03:56,106 --> 00:03:57,301
Pause the video now.

60
00:03:57,751 --> 00:04:01,847
Three times two is six. Six plus one is
seven. So x is seven.

61
00:04:02,297 --> 00:04:05,283
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

62
00:04:06,433 --> 00:04:10,337
Let's trace variables that change, line by
line, in a tiny game.

63
00:04:11,487 --> 00:04:15,199
The problem: a player starts with zero
points and three lives.

64
00:04:15,649 --> 00:04:20,252
They collect a coin worth ten, collect
another coin, then get hit and lose a

65
00:04:20,252 --> 00:04:22,796
life. What are score and lives at the end?

66
00:04:23,946 --> 00:04:26,698
Here's the code. Each line updates one
variable.

67
00:04:27,148 --> 00:04:32,054
score equals score plus ten takes the old
score, adds ten, and stores it back.

68
00:04:33,204 --> 00:04:36,511
Line one: score is zero. Line two: lives
is three.

69
00:04:36,961 --> 00:04:42,554
Line three: zero plus ten, so score is
ten. Line four: ten plus ten, score is

70
00:04:42,554 --> 00:04:43,062
twenty.

71
00:04:43,512 --> 00:04:48,248
Line five: three minus one, lives is two.
Line six prints twenty and two.

72
00:04:49,398 --> 00:04:51,105
The program prints twenty, two.

73
00:04:51,555 --> 00:04:57,024
A trace table is the best way to follow
variables. Each row shows the values right

74
00:04:57,024 --> 00:04:58,424
after that line runs.

75
00:04:59,574 --> 00:05:01,196
Your turn. What does this print?

76
00:05:01,646 --> 00:05:02,840
Pause the video now.

77
00:05:03,290 --> 00:05:06,938
Six and five. y got a copy of five before
x changed.

78
00:05:07,388 --> 00:05:08,668
Next, numbers and math.

79
00:05:09,819 --> 00:05:14,342
Computers are great at math. Python can
work like a powerful calculator.

80
00:05:14,792 --> 00:05:15,709
Let's see how.

81
00:05:16,859 --> 00:05:18,992
Python has two main kinds of numbers.

82
00:05:19,442 --> 00:05:24,731
Integers, or ints, are whole numbers, like
seven or negative two. Floats have a

83
00:05:24,731 --> 00:05:27,208
decimal point, like three point five.

84
00:05:28,358 --> 00:05:33,286
The basic operators are plus, minus, star
for times, and slash for divide.

85
00:05:33,736 --> 00:05:37,853
Slash always gives a float. Seven slash
two is three point five.

86
00:05:39,003 --> 00:05:44,313
Double slash is floor division. It divides
and drops the remainder. Seven double

87
00:05:44,313 --> 00:05:45,574
slash two is three.

88
00:05:46,024 --> 00:05:49,202
Percent gives the remainder. Seven percent
two is one.

89
00:05:50,352 --> 00:05:53,680
Double star is a power. Two double star
three is eight.

90
00:05:54,130 --> 00:05:59,384
Python follows the usual order of
operations: powers, then times and divide,

91
00:05:59,384 --> 00:06:03,325
then plus and minus. Use parentheses to
change the order.

92
00:06:04,475 --> 00:06:09,275
Percent has a handy use. A number percent
two is zero when the number is even.

93
00:06:09,725 --> 00:06:14,945
And seconds double slash sixty gives whole
minutes, while seconds percent sixty gives

94
00:06:14,945 --> 00:06:16,296
the seconds left over.

95
00:06:17,446 --> 00:06:21,776
Your turn. What is seventeen double slash
five, and seventeen percent five?

96
00:06:22,226 --> 00:06:23,421
Pause the video now.

97
00:06:23,871 --> 00:06:29,002
Five goes into seventeen three times, with
two left over. So the answers are three

98
00:06:29,002 --> 00:06:29,503
and two.

99
00:06:29,953 --> 00:06:32,940
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

100
00:06:34,089 --> 00:06:38,965
Let's use floor division and remainder to
solve a problem you see on every video

101
00:06:38,965 --> 00:06:39,209
app.

102
00:06:40,359 --> 00:06:45,277
The problem: a movie is one hundred twenty
five minutes long. Show it as hours and

103
00:06:45,277 --> 00:06:45,756
minutes.

104
00:06:46,906 --> 00:06:51,173
Double slash gives the whole hours.
Percent gives the minutes left over.

105
00:06:52,323 --> 00:06:57,390
One hundred twenty five double slash
sixty: sixty goes in twice, so hours is

106
00:06:57,390 --> 00:06:57,656
two.

107
00:06:58,106 --> 00:07:03,759
One hundred twenty five percent sixty: two
sixties make one twenty, leaving five. So

108
00:07:03,759 --> 00:07:04,634
mins is five.

109
00:07:05,784 --> 00:07:07,683
The output is two h, five min.

110
00:07:08,133 --> 00:07:13,896
Common mistake: using a single slash. One
twenty five slash sixty is two point zero

111
00:07:13,896 --> 00:07:15,770
eight three, not two hours.

112
00:07:16,920 --> 00:07:21,740
Your turn. You have seventeen cookies to
share among five friends. Each gets how

113
00:07:21,740 --> 00:07:23,427
many, and how many are left?

114
00:07:23,877 --> 00:07:25,072
Pause the video now.

115
00:07:25,522 --> 00:07:28,060
Each friend gets three, and two are left
over.

116
00:07:28,510 --> 00:07:29,534
Next, strings.

117
00:07:30,685 --> 00:07:34,184
Names, messages, passwords. Most programs
handle text.

118
00:07:34,634 --> 00:07:36,319
In Python, text is a string.

119
00:07:37,469 --> 00:07:42,495
A string is a sequence of characters
inside quotes. Single or double quotes

120
00:07:42,495 --> 00:07:43,165
both work.

121
00:07:43,615 --> 00:07:48,330
The len function tells you how many
characters a string has, including spaces.

122
00:07:49,480 --> 00:07:53,021
Plus joins strings together. This is
called concatenation.

123
00:07:53,471 --> 00:07:56,159
Hello plus a space plus Sam gives Hello
Sam.

124
00:07:56,609 --> 00:07:59,958
Star repeats a string. Ha times three
gives ha ha ha.

125
00:08:01,108 --> 00:08:05,674
Each character has a position, called an
index. Indexes start at zero.

126
00:08:06,124 --> 00:08:12,016
In the word python, index zero is p, and
index five is n. Negative one means the

127
00:08:12,016 --> 00:08:13,121
last character.

128
00:08:14,271 --> 00:08:19,391
A slice takes part of a string. word,
bracket, zero colon three, gives the

129
00:08:19,391 --> 00:08:22,228
characters at indexes zero, one, and two.

130
00:08:22,678 --> 00:08:24,470
The end index is not included.

131
00:08:25,620 --> 00:08:28,372
Strings have methods, actions you call
with a dot.

132
00:08:28,822 --> 00:08:33,814
upper makes everything capital. lower
makes everything small. replace swaps one

133
00:08:33,814 --> 00:08:35,457
piece of text for another.

134
00:08:36,607 --> 00:08:41,066
Your turn. word equals planet. What is
word, bracket, one colon four?

135
00:08:41,516 --> 00:08:42,710
Pause the video now.

136
00:08:43,160 --> 00:08:46,766
Indexes one, two and three: l, a, n. So
the answer is lan.

137
00:08:47,216 --> 00:08:50,202
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

138
00:08:51,352 --> 00:08:56,813
Let's slice and join strings to build a
username, a job real sign-up pages do.

139
00:08:57,963 --> 00:09:02,301
The problem: build a username from the
first three letters of a first name, the

140
00:09:02,301 --> 00:09:04,278
whole last name, and the birth year.

141
00:09:04,728 --> 00:09:06,883
For Sam Rivera, born in twenty eleven.

142
00:09:08,033 --> 00:09:12,214
Slice first from zero to three, add last,
and add the year as a string.

143
00:09:12,664 --> 00:09:14,499
Then lower makes it all lowercase.

144
00:09:15,649 --> 00:09:19,510
Here the first name is Samuel. first, zero
to three, is S A M.

145
00:09:19,960 --> 00:09:23,885
Join with Rivera, then twenty eleven: Sam
Rivera twenty eleven.

146
00:09:24,335 --> 00:09:27,877
lower makes it all lowercase. It's
thirteen characters long.

147
00:09:29,027 --> 00:09:32,397
The program prints sam rivera twenty
eleven, and thirteen.

148
00:09:32,847 --> 00:09:38,022
Remember: a slice stops before the end
index, so zero to three gives indexes

149
00:09:38,022 --> 00:09:39,247
zero, one and two.

150
00:09:40,397 --> 00:09:45,453
Your turn. word equals computer. What are
word, bracket zero to four, and word,

151
00:09:45,453 --> 00:09:46,797
bracket negative one?

152
00:09:47,247 --> 00:09:48,442
Pause the video now.

153
00:09:48,892 --> 00:09:49,809
comp, and r.

154
00:09:50,259 --> 00:09:51,198
Next, input.

155
00:09:52,348 --> 00:09:55,569
So far, our programs do the same thing
every time.

156
00:09:56,019 --> 00:09:58,195
Input lets the user change what happens.

157
00:09:59,345 --> 00:10:04,048
The input function shows a prompt, waits
for the user to type, and gives back what

158
00:10:04,048 --> 00:10:04,679
they typed.

159
00:10:05,129 --> 00:10:08,862
name equals input, what's your name. Then
print hello plus name.

160
00:10:10,012 --> 00:10:14,940
Here's the catch. input always gives back
a string, even if the user types a number.

161
00:10:15,390 --> 00:10:19,187
If the user types five, you get the string
five, not the number five.

162
00:10:20,337 --> 00:10:25,236
To do math, convert it. int of the input
gives a whole number. float of the input

163
00:10:25,236 --> 00:10:26,204
gives a decimal.

164
00:10:26,654 --> 00:10:29,107
age equals int of input, how old are you.

165
00:10:30,257 --> 00:10:35,122
Let's build a program. Ask for the user's
age, add one, and print, next year you'll

166
00:10:35,122 --> 00:10:36,295
be, and the new age.

167
00:10:36,745 --> 00:10:42,202
To join a number with text, convert it
with str, or pass both to print separated

168
00:10:42,202 --> 00:10:42,953
by a comma.

169
00:10:44,103 --> 00:10:48,625
If the user types something that isn't a
number, int will crash with a value error.

170
00:10:49,075 --> 00:10:51,934
Later, we'll learn how to check input
before using it.

171
00:10:53,084 --> 00:10:58,521
Your turn. The user types seven. What does
this print? x equals input. print x plus

172
00:10:58,521 --> 00:10:58,652
x.

173
00:10:59,102 --> 00:11:00,297
Pause the video now.

174
00:11:00,747 --> 00:11:05,772
input gives the string seven. Adding two
strings joins them, so it prints seventy

175
00:11:05,772 --> 00:11:06,144
seven.

176
00:11:06,594 --> 00:11:09,581
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

177
00:11:10,731 --> 00:11:15,510
Let's write a program that asks the user
for numbers, converts them, and calculates

178
00:11:15,510 --> 00:11:16,086
an answer.

179
00:11:17,236 --> 00:11:21,875
The problem: ask for a restaurant bill and
a tip percent, then print the tip and the

180
00:11:21,875 --> 00:11:22,206
total.

181
00:11:22,656 --> 00:11:25,792
The user types forty for the bill, and
fifteen for the tip.

182
00:11:26,942 --> 00:11:29,780
input gives back strings, so wrap each one
in float.

183
00:11:30,230 --> 00:11:35,811
The tip is the bill times percent over one
hundred. Use str to join numbers with

184
00:11:35,811 --> 00:11:36,160
text.

185
00:11:37,310 --> 00:11:43,109
The user types forty: bill becomes forty
point zero. Then fifteen: pct becomes

186
00:11:43,109 --> 00:11:44,521
fifteen point zero.

187
00:11:44,971 --> 00:11:51,061
tip is forty times fifteen, divided by a
hundred: six point zero. total is forty

188
00:11:51,061 --> 00:11:52,203
six point zero.

189
00:11:53,353 --> 00:11:58,281
The program prints Tip: six point zero,
and Total: forty six point zero.

190
00:11:58,731 --> 00:12:03,806
Common mistake: forgetting float. Then
bill is the string forty, and multiplying

191
00:12:03,806 --> 00:12:06,027
gives an error or a strange result.

192
00:12:07,177 --> 00:12:10,761
Your turn. A user types seven at this
prompt. What does it print?

193
00:12:11,211 --> 00:12:12,406
Pause the video now.

194
00:12:12,856 --> 00:12:16,568
Seven seven. n is the string seven, and
plus joins strings.

195
00:12:17,018 --> 00:12:18,042
Next, Booleans.

196
00:12:19,191 --> 00:12:23,948
Programs need to ask questions. Is the
password right? Is the score high enough?

197
00:12:24,398 --> 00:12:27,620
The answer is always one of two values:
True or False.

198
00:12:28,770 --> 00:12:32,823
In Python, True and False are Booleans.
They start with a capital letter.

199
00:12:33,273 --> 00:12:35,108
Comparisons produce Booleans.

200
00:12:36,258 --> 00:12:38,626
Python has six comparison operators.

201
00:12:39,076 --> 00:12:43,873
Less than, greater than, less than or
equal, greater than or equal, double

202
00:12:43,873 --> 00:12:47,438
equals for equal, and exclamation equals
for not equal.

203
00:12:48,588 --> 00:12:52,450
Remember: one equals sign assigns. Two
equals signs compare.

204
00:12:52,900 --> 00:12:57,614
x equals five stores a value. x double
equals five asks a question.

205
00:12:58,764 --> 00:13:01,516
You can combine questions with and, or,
and not.

206
00:13:01,966 --> 00:13:06,875
And is True when both sides are True. Or
is True when at least one side is True.

207
00:13:06,875 --> 00:13:08,409
Not flips True and False.

208
00:13:09,559 --> 00:13:14,911
For example, age greater than or equal to
thirteen, and age less than or equal to

209
00:13:14,911 --> 00:13:17,026
nineteen, is True for teenagers.

210
00:13:17,476 --> 00:13:22,891
Python can also write it as thirteen less
than or equal to age, less than or equal

211
00:13:22,891 --> 00:13:23,684
to nineteen.

212
00:13:24,834 --> 00:13:29,356
Your turn. x is four. What is x greater
than three and x less than four?

213
00:13:29,806 --> 00:13:31,001
Pause the video now.

214
00:13:31,451 --> 00:13:36,423
x greater than three is True, but x less
than four is False. True and False is

215
00:13:36,423 --> 00:13:36,806
False.

216
00:13:37,256 --> 00:13:40,242
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

217
00:13:41,393 --> 00:13:46,300
Let's combine comparisons with and, or and
not, and test them on real values.

218
00:13:47,450 --> 00:13:52,568
The problem: a museum gives a discount to
teens, ages thirteen to nineteen, or

219
00:13:52,568 --> 00:13:54,340
anyone with a student card.

220
00:13:54,790 --> 00:13:58,246
Write one Boolean expression, and test it
on three visitors.

221
00:13:59,396 --> 00:14:03,279
The age part needs and: at least thirteen,
and at most nineteen.

222
00:14:03,729 --> 00:14:06,865
Then or with the card. Parentheses make
the order clear.

223
00:14:08,015 --> 00:14:12,943
Visitor one: age twenty two, with a card.
teen is False, but card is True, so

224
00:14:12,943 --> 00:14:14,031
discount is True.

225
00:14:14,481 --> 00:14:18,897
Visitor two: age fifteen, no card. teen is
True, so discount is True.

226
00:14:19,347 --> 00:14:23,592
Visitor three: age forty, no card. Both
are False, so no discount.

227
00:14:24,742 --> 00:14:26,897
Only visitor three misses the discount.

228
00:14:27,347 --> 00:14:33,114
Common mistake: writing thirteen less than
or equal to age, or nineteen. Each side of

229
00:14:33,114 --> 00:14:35,624
and and or must be a full comparison.

230
00:14:36,774 --> 00:14:41,894
Your turn. With x equal to seven, what is
not x greater than five, or x double

231
00:14:41,894 --> 00:14:42,748
equals seven?

232
00:14:43,198 --> 00:14:44,392
Pause the video now.

233
00:14:44,842 --> 00:14:50,173
True. not x greater than five is False,
but x equals seven is True, and or needs

234
00:14:50,173 --> 00:14:50,773
only one.

235
00:14:51,223 --> 00:14:52,930
Next, let's put Part 1 together.

236
00:14:54,079 --> 00:14:57,578
You've learned print, variables, math,
strings, input and Booleans.

237
00:14:58,028 --> 00:15:02,572
Now let's use all of them in one complete
Python program that really runs.

238
00:15:03,722 --> 00:15:05,770
Here's the plan: a road trip planner.

239
00:15:06,220 --> 00:15:10,941
It asks for the trip details, works out
the gas and the cost, splits it between

240
00:15:10,941 --> 00:15:13,452
riders, and tells you if it's a long trip.

241
00:15:14,602 --> 00:15:17,418
Part one: print a title, then ask for the
details.

242
00:15:17,868 --> 00:15:23,001
name stays a string. Miles, miles per
gallon and price become floats. riders is

243
00:15:23,001 --> 00:15:24,950
a whole number, so we use int.

244
00:15:26,100 --> 00:15:30,388
Part two: the math. Gallons is miles
divided by miles per gallon.

245
00:15:30,838 --> 00:15:35,958
Cost is gallons times the price, and each
person pays cost divided by riders.

246
00:15:37,108 --> 00:15:42,142
Part three: print the results. upper
shouts the name. round keeps two decimals

247
00:15:42,142 --> 00:15:44,916
for money, and str turns numbers into
text.

248
00:15:45,366 --> 00:15:49,441
The last line prints a Boolean: is the
trip over three hundred miles?

249
00:15:50,591 --> 00:15:55,185
Let's run it. Maya is driving three
hundred sixty miles, at thirty miles per

250
00:15:55,185 --> 00:15:58,570
gallon, with gas at three eighty nine, and
three people.

251
00:15:59,020 --> 00:16:04,236
Twelve gallons, forty six dollars sixty
eight in total, fifteen fifty six each.

252
00:16:04,236 --> 00:16:05,953
And yes, it's a long trip.

253
00:16:07,103 --> 00:16:12,131
Your turn. One hundred fifty miles, twenty
five miles per gallon, four dollar gas,

254
00:16:12,131 --> 00:16:15,871
two people. What does each person pay, and
is it a long trip?

255
00:16:16,321 --> 00:16:17,516
Pause the video now.

256
00:16:17,966 --> 00:16:23,317
Six gallons costs twenty four dollars, so
twelve dollars each. It's not over three

257
00:16:23,317 --> 00:16:25,731
hundred miles, so long trip is False.

258
00:16:26,181 --> 00:16:30,384
That's Part 1. Next, Part 2: decisions,
loops, functions and lists.

